  "question": {
    "text": "The one which is least basic is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "mu8l3",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "bz6jf",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{NH}_{2}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "G25lP",
      "text": "$\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5}\\right)_{3} \\mathrm{~N}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "mE73E",
      "text": "$\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{NH}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "More the electron density on $\\mathrm{N}$, higher will be the basicity. Density on $\\mathrm{N}$ is influenced by the (i) nature of the group (+I or -I) present in alkyl group or benzene nucleus and (ii) resonance (delocalisation of the electron present on $\\mathrm{N}$ ). In $\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5}\\right)_{3} \\mathrm{~N}$ :\nelectron pair is delocalised to the maximum extent due to three benzene rings and hence least available for protonation, thus it will be least basic)",
    "image": null
  },
